<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0599">Example <span data-tag="0599">4.7.9.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ denote its homotopy category (Definition <a data-tag="004C" href="/tag/004C">1.4.5.3</a>). Then the comparison map $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} )$ of Construction <a data-tag="004G" href="/tag/004G">1.4.5.6</a> exhibits $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} )$ as a homotopy $1$-category of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0597" href="/tag/0597">4.7.9.1</a>. This is a reformulation of Proposition <a data-tag="004H" href="/tag/004H">1.4.5.7</a> (see Example <a data-tag="057G" href="/tag/057G">4.7.7.4</a>). Stated more informally, there is a canonical isomorphism of simplicial sets $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}1}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}} \simeq \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} )$. We will sometimes abuse notation by identifying the homotopy $1$-category $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}1}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}$ with the ordinary category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. </p>
</article>
